1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,360
The eyes of the world are on Paris and France.

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:09,680
So how better than for us to contribute a series on what is probably, Dominic,

3
00:00:09,820 --> 00:00:16,120
I mean, one of the great seismic events, not just in European, but in global history.

4
00:00:16,260 --> 00:00:19,840
Certainly one of the two or three greatest stories in world history.

5
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:26,820
I mean, the rollercoaster of bloodshed, violence, utopianism, insurrection, excitement, you name it.

6
00:00:26,820 --> 00:00:32,060
So this is the kickoff for our first series, which will take us through the first couple of years of the revolution.

7
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:35,700
Yeah, so we can look at some of the causes, some of the background to the revolution,

8
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:43,120
and then we'll take through the storming of the Bastille and go up to the point where the king, Louis XVI,

9
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:48,300
and his queen, Marie Antoinette, who we've just been hearing about, try to get out of France.

10
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:51,500
And spoiler alert, they fail.

11
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,400
And that is the cliffhanger on which we will leave it.

12
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:56,660
Yeah, because, of course, everybody knows.

13
00:00:56,820 --> 00:00:58,640
What will then happen to the king and queen?

14
00:00:58,920 --> 00:00:59,280
Yes.

15
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:05,960
And I think Marie Antoinette is a kind of wonderful focus for looking at some of the causes,

16
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:08,400
because she's very, very celebrated.

17
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:11,820
I mean, one of the instantly kind of recognisable figures.

18
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:15,740
She must be one of the two or three most well-known people in all French history, I would have said.

19
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:16,040
Yes.

20
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:26,340
And at the time, she is seen by enthusiasts for the revolution as the absolute embodiment of everything that prompted it.

21
00:01:26,820 --> 00:01:35,500
You know, she is the kind of the incarnation of Ancien Régime, arrogance, of aristocratic splendour,

22
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:41,600
and of a kind of quality of vampirism that has leached the French people of what should be rightfully theirs.

23
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:45,460
And in her lifetime, before and then particularly after the revolution,

24
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:50,860
she is the object of absolutely kind of obsessive hatred.

25
00:01:51,060 --> 00:01:56,380
And it's also a kind of prurient hatred, you know, because it is tied up with kind of fantasies.

26
00:01:56,380 --> 00:01:58,380
About what she's been getting up to.

27
00:01:58,680 --> 00:02:02,800
And I guess that that, I mean, that runs through to the present day,

28
00:02:02,900 --> 00:02:06,080
because the one phrase that everyone would associate with her,

29
00:02:06,120 --> 00:02:08,720
let them eat cake, is still repeated, isn't it?

30
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:11,560
Despite the fact that she never said it.

31
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:11,880
I know.

32
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:14,480
And the phrase, the original phrase is qu'ils mangent de la brioche.

33
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:18,280
So let them eat brioche has slightly different connotations, doesn't it?

34
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:23,760
Yeah, because I always kind of imagine them, you know, a big gato, strawberries on the top or something.

35
00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:26,360
But she never said it anyway, so there's no point in dissecting it, I guess.

36
00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:34,220
To some degree, other than the fact that the fact that people think she said it is indicative of this idea of her and Louis,

37
00:02:34,460 --> 00:02:37,620
who's supposedly kind of both, who's her puppet,

38
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:44,320
then living this life of unbelievable depravity and licentiousness and arrogance and not caring.

39
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:49,940
All of which, well, not all of it is completely wrong, but there are definitely huge problems with that.

40
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:50,860
Nuances there.

41
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:55,200
But again, I mean, against that, against the image of Marie Antoinette,

42
00:02:55,200 --> 00:03:04,040
as a kind of depraved vampire, there's also been right from the beginning for conservatives,

43
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:10,820
a sense that she is, you know, she is the archetype of the victims of the revolution.

44
00:03:11,780 --> 00:03:20,340
Her fate exemplifies what critics of the revolution see as its destructiveness, as its kind of bloodlust.

45
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:23,540
So the revolution itself is cast as a vampire.

46
00:03:23,540 --> 00:03:34,440
And in England, the person who classically first articulates that is the Irish politician and Whig,

47
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:41,120
so not even a Tory, Edmund Burke, who November 1790 wrote reflections on the revolution in France.

48
00:03:41,340 --> 00:03:48,220
And in that, he famously recalls seeing Marie Antoinette kind of 16 or 17 years before at the court,

49
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:52,620
glittering like the morning star full of life and splendor and joy.

50
00:03:52,620 --> 00:03:56,680
And he famously enthuses,

51
00:03:56,860 --> 00:04:02,600
I thought 10,000 swords must have leaped from their scabbards to avenge even a look that threatened her with insult.

52
00:04:02,820 --> 00:04:04,920
But the age of chivalry is gone.

53
00:04:04,980 --> 00:04:08,000
That of sophisters, economists and calculators has succeeded.

54
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:11,780
And the glory of Europe is extinguished forever.

55
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:16,780
I would say his is the, among biographers and among people who studied the revolution,

56
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:22,060
it's his take actually that is more enduring than the,

57
00:04:22,060 --> 00:04:26,180
than the let them eat cake, she's a vampire school of thought, isn't it?

58
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:30,600
Because actually now it's, I would say most writers are filled with horror

59
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:32,540
when they look at what happened to Marie Antoinette,

60
00:04:32,680 --> 00:04:36,740
this young girl who arrived in France and was turned into a hate figure for the mob.

61
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:39,800
Chantal Thomas, who we opened with,

62
00:04:39,960 --> 00:04:46,920
I mean, her book is about the vituperation that is kind of lashed on Marie Antoinette.

63
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:51,880
And she has a section where she talks about how biographers through the 19th and into the 20th century,

64
00:04:52,060 --> 00:04:53,760
there was a debate about whether these were true or not.

65
00:04:53,880 --> 00:04:58,060
And she just says, you know, none of these stories about the incest and the vampirism are true.

66
00:04:58,160 --> 00:04:59,340
I mean, they're obviously not true.

67
00:04:59,480 --> 00:05:03,660
And she says that most recent biographies have been written,

68
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:06,500
and to quote her, with sympathy, if not with love.

69
00:05:06,760 --> 00:05:09,880
And I would say that's probably true of her as well,

70
00:05:09,940 --> 00:05:14,940
because she wrote a novel that then got turned into a film called Farewell My Queen in English

71
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:16,860
that featured Diane Kruger.

72
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,200
So Helen of Troy and Troy.

73
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:21,980
As Marie Antoinette.

74
00:05:22,260 --> 00:05:24,680
And that's a French film.

75
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:28,360
But of course, Marie Antoinette has also been a kind of an American pinup.

76
00:05:28,820 --> 00:05:33,440
So a film that was massively popular with my daughters when they were growing up,

77
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:36,080
came out in 2006, is Sofia Coppola's Marie Antoinette,

78
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:40,320
in which she's kind of cast as, you know, very much a contemporary heroine.

79
00:05:40,380 --> 00:05:41,900
She's surrounded by her squad.

80
00:05:42,180 --> 00:05:45,580
She's got converse trainers in the cabinet.

81
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:49,420
You know, she rushes around Versailles to new wave bands.

82
00:05:49,420 --> 00:05:52,120
And what was interesting about that,

83
00:05:52,240 --> 00:05:54,580
I was kind of reading up about the reception of it in France,

84
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:56,520
because it was premiered at Cannes.

85
00:05:57,380 --> 00:06:01,360
And there it was greeted with boos and then with cheers.

86
00:06:01,460 --> 00:06:05,160
So kind of answering boos and cheers by the French journalists who'd been watching it,

87
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:10,300
which I guess kind of sums up a massive division in France itself.

88
00:06:10,440 --> 00:06:14,140
Because Marie Antoinette stands for the revolution to some degree,

89
00:06:14,280 --> 00:06:15,620
because what you think of Marie Antoinette,

90
00:06:16,210 --> 00:06:19,180
you know, you ask a French, an educated Frenchman,

91
00:06:19,420 --> 00:06:20,460
what they think of Marie Antoinette,

92
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:24,500
and their answer will tell you, well, they think of the revolution probably,

93
00:06:25,120 --> 00:06:30,140
and probably also where they stand politically even today in the 21st century,

94
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:30,620
don't you think?

95
00:06:30,860 --> 00:06:32,160
Yeah, I really, I mean, I do.

96
00:06:32,300 --> 00:06:36,240
And I think to a degree that may be true as well in the broader world,

97
00:06:36,300 --> 00:06:36,920
perhaps a bit.

98
00:06:37,040 --> 00:06:38,940
I mean, it's much more focused in France, obviously.

99
00:06:40,220 --> 00:06:44,120
And I think that that sense of ambivalence,

100
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:47,760
you know, is Marie Antoinette a monster or is she a victim?

101
00:06:47,760 --> 00:06:51,540
Is she someone to be detested and loathed?

102
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:53,620
Or is she someone to be pitied and admired?

103
00:06:54,440 --> 00:06:57,760
That's an ambivalence that is there even before the revolution.

104
00:06:58,100 --> 00:07:02,740
So what is the way that she is viewed by the French public

105
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:06,280
is a kind of mixture of loathing and desire.

106
00:07:06,860 --> 00:07:08,200
Because she's not always hated.

107
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:09,800
That's an important point to make.

108
00:07:09,880 --> 00:07:10,180
No, she's not.

109
00:07:10,280 --> 00:07:14,320
And there are certain, you know, she's very popular with certain segments of society.

110
00:07:14,820 --> 00:07:16,260
And I think that

111
00:07:17,120 --> 00:07:21,060
she's cast either as a kind of emblem of royal grace,

112
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:25,860
because she's, as we will see, in a way, she's a very, very modern figure.

113
00:07:25,940 --> 00:07:26,480
Yeah, completely.

114
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:30,340
She kind of basically, I think, invents the French ideal of fashion.

115
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:32,260
She's way ahead of the game.

116
00:07:32,440 --> 00:07:37,160
But to other people, this makes her the kind of the embodiment of frivolity or decadence.

117
00:07:37,380 --> 00:07:38,880
She's a very proactive queen.

118
00:07:39,100 --> 00:07:43,860
Queens in France generally are not expected to play a proactive role in court life

119
00:07:43,860 --> 00:07:45,700
and national life, still less national life.

120
00:07:45,700 --> 00:07:47,440
Marie Antoinette comes to do that.

121
00:07:47,840 --> 00:07:54,180
And she's a kind of emblem of all the paradoxes that we'll be talking about

122
00:07:54,180 --> 00:07:55,300
in the context of the revolution.

123
00:07:55,660 --> 00:08:00,360
But she is, the fact that she's a figure of fantasy is really crucial

124
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:06,740
because not just her, but the revolution itself is about the creation of stories,

125
00:08:06,820 --> 00:08:14,100
the projection of stories and narratives onto facts of the economy, agriculture,

126
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:18,140
the crowds, the court, everything.

127
00:08:18,340 --> 00:08:22,920
It's how you structure the story that basically determines your attitudes to the revolution, I think.

128
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:23,560
Do you think that's...

129
00:08:23,560 --> 00:08:24,800
I absolutely, yeah, totally agree, Tom.

130
00:08:24,940 --> 00:08:29,300
I mean, historians like Simon Sharma and Robert Danton have written brilliant books

131
00:08:29,300 --> 00:08:31,460
about how the French Revolution is there.

132
00:08:31,920 --> 00:08:35,220
It's a sort of hall of mirrors or a hall of narratives, I suppose.

133
00:08:35,480 --> 00:08:37,500
Very appropriate for Versailles.

134
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:38,160
For Versailles, exactly.

135
00:08:38,280 --> 00:08:42,120
So let's start not at Versailles, but with Marie Antoinette herself.

136
00:08:42,660 --> 00:08:43,720
So she is born.

137
00:08:43,720 --> 00:08:45,380
She was born, Tom, in 1755.

138
00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:48,980
And of course, the single most important thing about her, I would say,

139
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:50,940
is that she is not French.

140
00:08:51,360 --> 00:08:52,880
She is Austrian.

141
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:53,720
She is...

142
00:08:53,720 --> 00:08:55,880
We love the Habsburgs on The Rest Is History.

143
00:08:55,920 --> 00:09:00,560
I don't think there'll ever be a historical podcast in the history of mankind

144
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:05,040
that is so keen on the Habsburgs as The Rest Is History.

145
00:09:05,140 --> 00:09:07,200
We've had Edward Habsburg talking about the Habsburgs.

146
00:09:07,200 --> 00:09:09,600
We've had Franz Ferdinand, great friend of The Rest Is History.

147
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:12,280
And now another Habsburg, Marie Antoinette.

148
00:09:12,280 --> 00:09:14,780
Yes, although she's born Maria Antonia.

149
00:09:15,580 --> 00:09:21,120
And I think that it's fair to say that her identity as an Austrian

150
00:09:21,120 --> 00:09:28,300
and as a Habsburg is kind of fundamental both to how her admirers see her

151
00:09:28,300 --> 00:09:30,280
and how her detractors see her.

152
00:09:30,720 --> 00:09:36,140
So to her admirers, she is an embodiment of a new age of peace

153
00:09:36,140 --> 00:09:40,760
between what traditionally have been the great rivals in European politics.

154
00:09:40,940 --> 00:09:41,120
Yeah.

155
00:09:41,160 --> 00:09:41,360
Yeah.

156
00:09:42,280 --> 00:09:42,740
And it's interesting that, you know,

157
00:09:42,740 --> 00:09:45,000
in the late 19th century, Charles V and François I,

158
00:09:45,120 --> 00:09:49,760
you know, these are the people who are kind of tearing Europe up

159
00:09:49,760 --> 00:09:51,860
while Luther is busy getting on with his stuff.

160
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:57,640
And France and the Habsburg Empire have been the great geopolitical rivals

161
00:09:57,640 --> 00:09:58,780
on the continent of Europe.

162
00:09:59,760 --> 00:10:03,600
But this has changed over the course of the 18th century

163
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:08,100
due to the emergence of two new great powers.

164
00:10:08,300 --> 00:10:08,380
Yeah.

165
00:10:08,460 --> 00:10:10,620
One of those is Britain, very happy to say.

166
00:10:11,920 --> 00:10:14,940
So in the Seven Years' War, Britain is taking on France

167
00:10:14,940 --> 00:10:17,640
and winning as a kind of global power.

168
00:10:18,020 --> 00:10:20,560
But on the continent, there's the emergence of Prussia.

169
00:10:20,740 --> 00:10:21,220
Yes.

170
00:10:21,360 --> 00:10:27,440
And Prussia is hungry for a lot of Austria's traditional territories.

171
00:10:28,160 --> 00:10:30,740
And under its great king, Frederick II,

172
00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:34,380
it annexes a province called Silesia, doesn't it?

173
00:10:34,440 --> 00:10:36,940
Which is basically now, it's a chunk of Poland.

174
00:10:37,160 --> 00:10:37,280
Yeah.

175
00:10:37,320 --> 00:10:39,940
And the Habsburgs are terribly upset about this.

176
00:10:39,940 --> 00:10:43,320
And basically, they kind of enter into an alliance with France

177
00:10:43,320 --> 00:10:45,100
so that they can get Silesia back.

178
00:10:45,260 --> 00:10:47,220
And France enters into an alliance with Austria

179
00:10:47,220 --> 00:10:49,620
so that she can have a counterweight to Britain.

180
00:10:50,020 --> 00:10:52,340
So the diplomatic revolution historians call this,

181
00:10:52,360 --> 00:10:55,640
because it's such a seismic moment in European history.

182
00:10:55,840 --> 00:10:55,960
Yeah.

183
00:10:56,020 --> 00:10:58,680
So the Seven Years' War breaks out in 1756

184
00:11:00,020 --> 00:11:03,340
with Britain and Prussia against Austria and France.

185
00:11:03,740 --> 00:11:08,580
And the following year, a very heavyweight figure in French politics,

186
00:11:08,820 --> 00:11:09,740
the Duc de Choiseul,

187
00:11:09,740 --> 00:11:12,420
arrives in Vienna as the French ambassador.

188
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:17,960
And the rulers who greet him, they're a couple, aren't they?

189
00:11:18,080 --> 00:11:20,320
So the emperor is Francis I.

190
00:11:20,960 --> 00:11:24,960
But the person who's really kind of cracking the whip

191
00:11:24,960 --> 00:11:28,080
is his wife, the empress, Maria Theresa.

192
00:11:28,580 --> 00:11:32,700
And she is a very, very proficient politician.

193
00:11:33,060 --> 00:11:34,520
Indeed, one might say stateswoman.

194
00:11:34,960 --> 00:11:37,000
But she's also very, very fertile.

195
00:11:37,300 --> 00:11:39,480
And the Habsburgs have always, I mean,

196
00:11:39,480 --> 00:11:40,600
it's been their boast, isn't it,

197
00:11:40,620 --> 00:11:43,280
that some empires get their way by fighting.

198
00:11:43,400 --> 00:11:45,000
We do it by marrying off our children.

199
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:48,980
And Maria Antonia is their 11th daughter

200
00:11:48,980 --> 00:11:51,540
and, as it will turn out, their last daughter.

201
00:11:52,420 --> 00:11:56,460
And the reason that the Duc de Choiseul is interested in her

202
00:11:56,460 --> 00:11:58,480
is that she is one year younger

203
00:11:58,480 --> 00:12:01,840
than the grandson of Louis XV, the French king,

204
00:12:02,040 --> 00:12:03,560
also inevitably called Louis.

205
00:12:03,800 --> 00:12:05,980
And his very fertile mind is thinking,

206
00:12:06,140 --> 00:12:07,840
well, we could, you know,

207
00:12:07,840 --> 00:12:09,480
we could maybe marry them off.

208
00:12:09,580 --> 00:12:10,820
A brilliant way to cement the alliance

209
00:12:10,820 --> 00:12:12,620
and to ensure that the alliance endures.

210
00:12:13,060 --> 00:12:13,540
Yes.

211
00:12:13,720 --> 00:12:15,080
And so in due course,

212
00:12:15,700 --> 00:12:17,780
Choiseul goes back from Vienna.

213
00:12:17,900 --> 00:12:18,660
He goes back to Paris

214
00:12:18,660 --> 00:12:19,840
and he becomes the foreign minister,

215
00:12:20,280 --> 00:12:21,280
basically the kind of the,

216
00:12:22,240 --> 00:12:25,440
almost the prime minister for Louis XV.

217
00:12:26,260 --> 00:12:28,140
And he's kind of pushing this idea.

218
00:12:28,340 --> 00:12:29,580
He's saying, you know,

219
00:12:29,600 --> 00:12:32,700
we should really try and cement this alliance.

220
00:12:33,680 --> 00:12:37,120
And it comes into further focus in 1765

221
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:40,360
when Louis XV's son

222
00:12:40,360 --> 00:12:44,660
and therefore the father of this guy who,

223
00:12:44,740 --> 00:12:45,240
this Louis,

224
00:12:45,380 --> 00:12:48,980
who is being lined up to marry Maria Antonia,

225
00:12:49,100 --> 00:12:49,920
he dies.

226
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:52,260
And so the grandson of Louis XV

227
00:12:52,260 --> 00:12:53,600
is now the Dauphin,

228
00:12:53,740 --> 00:12:56,280
the kind of French equivalent of the Prince of Wales,

229
00:12:56,380 --> 00:12:57,300
the heir to the throne.

230
00:12:57,360 --> 00:12:57,880
He's 11.

231
00:12:58,120 --> 00:13:01,940
So he's 11 and Maria Antonia is then what?

232
00:13:02,020 --> 00:13:02,680
Sorry, is she 10?

233
00:13:02,700 --> 00:13:03,180
10, yeah.

234
00:13:03,280 --> 00:13:05,060
On the 4th of April, 1770,

235
00:13:05,900 --> 00:13:06,760
it's all agreed.

236
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:08,580
The marriage contract is signed.

237
00:13:09,220 --> 00:13:10,300
There's a kind of weird thing

238
00:13:10,300 --> 00:13:12,040
where they do a proxy marriage in Vienna.

239
00:13:12,220 --> 00:13:13,380
So the new emperor,

240
00:13:13,540 --> 00:13:15,880
Maria Theresa's son, Joseph,

241
00:13:16,480 --> 00:13:20,460
he stands in for the Dauphin.

